## Decoding the question
**"Discuss the background"** asks for the **context** of each film — the social problem it addressed, the legal situation at the time, the story it told and why it mattered — not just a plot summary. The two films are ***Saving Face*** (Oscar ==2012==, acid attacks) and ***A Girl in the River: The Price of Forgiveness*** (Oscar ==2016==, honour killing). End with an evaluation of their impact.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Who she is
**Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy**, a journalist and documentary filmmaker from **Karachi**, is the first Pakistani to win an **Academy Award (Oscar)** — and she has won two, both for documentaries about **violence against women**.

#### ii) The argument
Each film grew out of a specific background: a form of violence that was common but hidden, laws that failed to punish it, and survivors whose stories had not been told. By telling those stories to the world, the films turned hidden crimes into **national debates** and helped push the state towards **law reform** — though their own stories show that laws alone do not end violence.

### 2. The filmmaker
Born in Karachi in ==1978==, Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy studied at **Smith College** and **Stanford University** in the United States. She began as a journalist and turned to documentary film to expose injustice. Her early films included ***Terror's Children*** (==2002==), about Afghan refugee children, and ***Pakistan: Children of the Taliban***, which won an **Emmy** in ==2010==. Through her company, **SOC Films**, she has focused on stories of women, children and minorities.

### 3. *Saving Face* — background: acid attacks in Pakistan
In the 2000s, **acid attacks** became a growing crime in Pakistan, especially in parts of southern **Punjab**, where acid used in cotton processing and other industries was cheap and easily available. Attackers threw acid, usually on a woman's face, in revenge for refusing a marriage proposal or sexual advances, or in domestic and family disputes. Victims were left **blind**, **disfigured** and socially isolated; many could not work, marry or appear in public, and needed repeated, expensive surgery.

### 4. *Saving Face* — the law before 2011
Before ==2011== there was no specific law on acid attacks: they were prosecuted as ordinary "hurt" under the Penal Code, with lighter penalties, and the sale of acid was unregulated. Perpetrators were rarely punished. Activists, the **Acid Survivors Foundation** and women legislators campaigned for a dedicated law, and a private member's bill moved through Parliament in 2011 — the period in which the film was made.

### 5. *Saving Face* — the story
***Saving Face***, co-directed with the American filmmaker **Daniel Junge**, follows **Dr Mohammad Jawad**, a British-Pakistani plastic surgeon who returned to Pakistan to perform reconstructive surgery on acid-attack survivors. It tells the stories of two women:
- **Zakia**, attacked by her husband, who pursues justice in court;
- **Rukhsana**, attacked by her husband and in-laws, who has to return to live with them because she has nowhere else to go.
The film shows both the cruelty of the attacks and the courage of survivors, doctors, lawyers and legislators working for change.

### 6. *Saving Face* — the Oscar and the Acid Act
*Saving Face* won the **Academy Award for Best Documentary (Short Subject)** in ==February 2012== — Pakistan's first Oscar. It coincided with the passage of the **Acid Control and Acid Crime Prevention Act 2011**, which added sections **336A and 336B** to the Penal Code and set punishments of **14 years to life imprisonment** and a minimum fine of **one million rupees**. The government awarded the filmmaker the **Hilal-i-Imtiaz**, and *TIME* named her among the world's most influential people.

::: fig compare | Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy's two Oscar-winning documentaries.
## Saving Face (2012)
- Problem: acid attacks on women
- Law before: no specific offence; acid sold freely
- Story: Dr Mohammad Jawad; survivors Zakia and Rukhsana
- Co-director: Daniel Junge
- Linked reform: Acid Control and Acid Crime Prevention Act 2011
## A Girl in the River (2016)
- Problem: honour killing
- Law before: heirs could forgive the killer
- Story: Saba Qaiser, 19, shot by father and uncle, survived
- Setting: near Gujranwala, Punjab
- Linked reform: honour-killing Act, October 2016
:::

### 7. *A Girl in the River* — background: honour killing and the forgiveness loophole
Hundreds of women are killed each year in Pakistan in the name of **honour** — at least **405** in ==2024==, according to the Human Rights Commission of Pakistan. Under the ***Qisas* and *Diyat*** law (==1990==, made an Act in ==1997==), murder was compoundable: the victim's **heirs** could forgive the killer. In honour killings, the heirs were usually the killer's own family, so fathers and brothers who killed daughters and sisters were "forgiven" and freed. A bill to close this loophole, moved by Senator **Sughra Imam** in ==2014==, had stalled.

### 8. *A Girl in the River* — the story of Saba Qaiser
***A Girl in the River: The Price of Forgiveness*** tells the story of **Saba Qaiser**, a 19-year-old from a village near **Gujranwala**, Punjab. In ==2014== she married the man she loved against her family's wishes. Her **father and uncle** shot her in the head, put her in a sack and threw her into a **river**. Miraculously she survived and dragged herself to the bank. The film follows her recovery and her pursuit of justice — and the intense pressure from elders and the community that led her, in the end, to **forgive** her attackers, who were then released. Her father declared that he had done the right thing.

### 9. *A Girl in the River* — the Oscar and the 2016 law
The film won the **Academy Award for Best Documentary (Short Subject)** in ==February 2016==. Prime Minister **Nawaz Sharif** hosted a screening and declared, "**There is no honour in honour killing**", promising to change the law. After the murder of **Qandeel Baloch** in ==July 2016==, Parliament passed the **Criminal Law (Amendment) (Offences in the name or pretext of Honour) Act** in ==October 2016==, under which a court must impose **life imprisonment** even if the family forgives the killer.

### 10. Common themes

#### i) Impunity
Both films show how families and communities shield perpetrators.

#### ii) Survivors' agency
Zakia, Rukhsana and Saba are not just victims; they resist, seek justice and rebuild their lives.

#### iii) Film as advocacy
Both films were made and screened at moments of legislative debate and were used to press for **law reform**.

#### iv) Two faces of Pakistan
Alongside cruelty, the films show Pakistani doctors, lawyers, activists and politicians working for change.

### 11. Criticism and response
Some critics in Pakistan accused her of "washing dirty linen in public" — tarnishing the country's image abroad by showing its worst problems to Western audiences — and of portraying Pakistani women only as victims. Her supporters reply that **silence protects abusers**, that the films highlight Pakistani reformers and survivors' courage, and that they produced concrete legal change at home. She has said her aim was to provoke debate inside Pakistan.

### 12. Evaluation and conclusion
Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy's two Oscar-winning documentaries were rooted in specific backgrounds: the unpunished epidemic of **acid attacks** and the **forgiveness loophole** that freed honour killers. By telling the stories of Zakia, Rukhsana and Saba Qaiser, they brought global attention to hidden crimes and contributed to the **Acid Act 2011** and the **honour-killing Act 2016**. Yet the films' own endings — Rukhsana returning to her abusers, Saba forced to forgive — show that **enforcement and social change** must follow law reform. Her work shows the power of media as a tool in the struggle against gender-based violence.

## Why this answer scores
- Gives the **background** of each film — problem, law, story, impact — exactly as asked.
- Uses accurate **names, dates and laws** (Junge, Jawad, Zakia, Rukhsana, Saba Qaiser, 2011, 2016).
- Adds **common themes**, **criticism** and an **evaluation**, lifting it above narration.
- A **compare figure** sets the two films side by side.
